# Sanjeevani patch template: unbounded copy into a fixed-size buffer.
#
# Fields are simple key: value. Multi-line values are not needed, which keeps
# the parser to a few lines - the templates are DATA, not code.

name: unbounded strcpy
cwe: CWE-121 stack buffer overflow

# Does the decompiled C look like this bug?
detect: \bstrcpy\s*\(

# What to rewrite. Groups are the destination and source.
#
# The obvious replacement is strncpy, and for SOURCE code it would be right.
# For BINARY patching it fails: the target only links against the functions it
# already imports, and a binary that called strcpy has no PLT entry for
# strncpy, so the patch will not link. The bounded copy is therefore written
# out inline - it calls nothing, so it always links.
find: strcpy\s*\(\s*(\w+)\s*,\s*(\w+)\s*\)
replace: { unsigned long _i; for (_i = 0; _i + 1 < sizeof(\1) && \2[_i]; _i++) \1[_i] = \2[_i]; \1[_i] = '\0'; }

explain: strcpy copies until it meets a NUL byte and has no idea how large the
 destination is. The replacement copies at most sizeof(dst) - 1 bytes and then
 terminates the string itself. It is written as an inline loop rather than a
 call to strncpy because a patched binary can only call functions it already
 imports.

# Honest note carried into the patch report. This is a real trade-off, not a
# detail to hide: the proof step will flag it on inputs the original survived.
caveat: this truncates inputs longer than the buffer. The original copied them
 whole (overflowing), so behaviour genuinely differs on long inputs.
